diff --git a/docs/development.md b/docs/development.md
index 0bca076..aa5b74c 100644
--- a/docs/development.md
+++ b/docs/development.md
@@ -72,8 +72,21 @@ bin/rails dev:email
 
 Under the hood, this will create or remove `tmp/email-dev.txt`.
 
+## Languages
+
+Fizzy speaks English and Japanese. English is what everybody gets until they choose otherwise.
+
+Adding `?locale=ja` to any URL switches that person to Japanese. The choice sticks for the rest of the session and is remembered on their identity, so it comes back the next time they sign in, even from a clean browser. Asking for a locale Fizzy doesn't speak (`?locale=xx`) changes nothing.
+
+Translations live in `config/locales/en.yml` and `config/locales/ja.yml`, plus `saas/config/locales` for the SaaS gem. Views use lazy lookups (`t(".title")`), everything else uses the full key. Strings the browser renders come from `t("javascript")`: they ship with the page as JSON and are read by `app/javascript/helpers/translations.js`.
+
+`test/i18n/translations_test.rb` keeps the languages honest: every key has to exist in every language, and every key the app asks for has to exist.
+
+The static error pages in `public/` are served before we know who is looking, so they are written in both languages.
+
 ## SaaS gem
 
+
 37signals bundles Fizzy with [`fizzy-saas`](https://github.com/basecamp/fizzy/tree/main/saas), a companion gem that links Fizzy with our billing system and contains our production setup.
 
 This gem depends on some private git repositories and it is not meant to be used by third parties. But we hope it can serve as inspiration for anyone wanting to run fizzy on their own infrastructure.
